course: world
lesson: 103
title: Morning, afternoon and the 24-hour clock
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A day has 24 hours, but an ordinary clock face shows only 12, so its short hand goes round twice a day: once from midnight to midday, and once from midday to midnight.
explain: To say which half you mean, people add "am" (before midday, from Latin "ante meridiem") or "pm" (after midday, "post meridiem"). Pick the wrong one and your 7:00 alarm rings at 7 in the evening, twelve hours off. "You picked pm, ya numpty!" ("numpty" is Scottish for a silly person).
explain: The 24-hour clock gets rid of am and pm: it counts the hours from 00 at midnight up to 23 late at night. Trains, planes, TV guides, cinemas and much of Europe use it. For any hour over 12, take away 12 to get the pm time: 15:30 is 3:30 pm, 20:00 is 8 pm.
explain: Small numbers like 01:00 to 05:00 are the middle of the night; numbers from 13 to 23 are afternoon and evening. Armies and pilots read 06:00 as "oh six hundred", which in a film just means 6 in the morning.
explain: When something runs across midnight, like a night shift from 22:00 to 06:00, split it in two: 2 hours up to midnight plus 6 hours after it makes 8 hours.
fact: An ordinary clock face shows 12 hours, so the hands go round twice in one day of 24 hours.
fact: "am" means before midday and "pm" means after midday; 7:00 am and 7:00 pm are 12 hours apart.
fact: The 24-hour clock counts hours from 00 at midnight to 23 at night, so it needs no am or pm.
fact: To change a 24-hour time over 12 into pm time, take away 12: 15:30 is 3:30 pm and 20:00 is 8:00 pm.
fact: Noon, or midday, is 12:00 on the 24-hour clock, and midnight is 00:00.
fact: Many people say 12 am for midnight and 12 pm for noon, but these muddle even grown-ups, so timetables often just write "noon" and "midnight".
fact: On the 24-hour clock, times from 13:00 to 23:59 are always afternoon or evening, so 13:45 is 1:45 pm.
fact: Armies, ships and pilots say 24-hour times as "hundred hours": 0600 is "oh six hundred", 6 am, and 1400 is "fourteen hundred", 2 pm.
fact: A night shift from 22:00 to 06:00 lasts 8 hours: 2 hours up to midnight and 6 hours after it.
fact: After a power cut, oven and microwave clocks often reset, and if the am or pm is set wrong the clock is 12 hours out.
fact: Timetables are exact to the minute: a bus at 07:52 leaves 8 minutes before 8, so rounding it to "eight-ish" makes you miss it.
fact: In France and much of Europe people say the 24-hour time in everyday talk: "dix-neuf heures" is 19:00, which is 7 pm.
fact: Words people use: "-ish" means roughly ("eight-ish" is about 8), "half eight" is 8:30 in Britain, and "graveyard shift" is slang for a very late night shift.
exercise: Ella sets her alarm for 7:00 but picks pm. When does it ring? | 7 in the evening | 7 in the morning | 7 at midnight | 7 minutes later
exercise: What does "am" mean? | Before midday | Any time after midday | Exactly at midnight | All morning long
exercise: At the station, Noah sees "Departs 15:30". What is that on a 12-hour clock? | 3:30 pm | 5:30 pm | 3:30 am | 1:30 pm
exercise: How many hours does the 24-hour clock count in one day? | 24, from 00 to 23 | 12, from 1 to 12 | 24, from 1 to 25 | 12, then it starts again at 13
exercise: What is midnight on the 24-hour clock? | 00:00 | 12:00 | 24:30 | 01:00
exercise: What is noon on the 24-hour clock? | 12:00 | 00:00 | 24:00 | 12:00 pm only
exercise: Kofi's match on TV is at 20:00 and his bedtime is half eight. How much of the match will he see? | 30 minutes | 1 hour 30 minutes | None of it at all | Only 12 minutes
exercise: A soldier in a film says, "We go at oh six hundred." When is that? | 6:00 in the morning | 6:00 in the early evening | 16:00 in the afternoon | 6 hundred minutes from now
exercise: Ava's cinema ticket says 13:45. When is the film? | 1:45 in the afternoon | 1:45 at night | 3:45 in the afternoon | 11:45 in the morning
exercise: Ben's dad works from 22:00 to 06:00. How long is the shift? | 8 hours | 16 hours | 6 hours | 4 hours
exercise: Gran's oven clock glows "11:00 AM" at 11 in the evening. How far out is it? | 12 hours | 1 hour | 11 hours | 24 hours
exercise: What should Gran's oven show at 11 in the evening on the 24-hour clock? | 23:00 | 11:00 | 13:00 | 21:00
exercise: Rhys's bus is at 07:52. He says "eight-ish" and turns up at 8:00. What happened? | He missed it by 8 minutes | He was 8 minutes early | He was right on time | He waited 52 minutes
exercise: In France a shop worker says they close at "dix-neuf heures". When is that? | 7 pm | 9 pm | 19 minutes past nine | 9 am
exercise: Why do trains and planes like the 24-hour clock? | It needs no am or pm, so there is less muddle | It makes every journey shorter | It counts minutes faster than other clocks | It is the only clock that works at night
test: Ella fumes, "Stupid phone rang at 7 at night!" Her sister says, "You picked pm, ya numpty." What did Ella do wrong? | She set pm, after midday, instead of am | She set the alarm on the wrong day | She set 17:00 instead of 07:00 | The phone was broken, not her
test: Noah asks, "Are there 15 o'clocks now?" at a timetable showing 15:30. What is the best answer? | Yes, on the 24-hour clock; it is 3:30 pm | No, 15:30 is a typing mistake for 1:30 | No, 15:30 means 15 minutes past 3 in the morning | Yes, and it means half past five
test: Zoe's dad says, "Midnight is 12 am, noon is 12 pm, don't ask me why." What is a clearer way to say these? | Say "midnight" and "noon", or 00:00 and 12:00 | Say 12 am for noon and 12 pm for midnight | Say 24 am and 24 pm | There is no clearer way to say them
test: Kofi, lowkey stressed (a bit worried, but hiding it): "Footy's at 20:00, bedtime half eight, is that past my bedtime?" | No, 20:00 is 8 pm, half an hour before bed | Yes, 20:00 is 10 pm, way past bedtime | Yes, 20:00 is 12 hours after bedtime | No, 20:00 is 2 pm, in the afternoon
test: Ava thinks 13:45 is "quarter to two at night". What's the right fix? | It is 1:45 in the afternoon | It is quarter to two in the morning | It is quarter to three at night | It is 11:45 in the late morning
test: Ben's dad, bleary on the graveyard shift: "Ten at night till six, how long's that, kid?" | 8 hours | 4 hours | 16 hours | 12 hours
test: Isla laughs, "Your oven thinks it's morning, Nan!" when it shows AM at 11 at night. What probably happened? | After a power cut it was reset with the wrong am or pm | Ovens always show the time in another country | The oven runs 12 hours fast every day | The oven only shows morning times
test: Rhys's mate, Gen Z and salty (annoyed): "Bus said 07:52, I said eight-ish, it was gone." What lesson is there? | Round bus times earlier, never later | Buses always leave late, so round up | 07:52 is the same as 8:00 on timetables | Timetables are never exact to the minute
test: Mila's dad hears "dix-neuf heures" and says, "Nineteen hours? That's tomorrow!" What's the right answer? | It means 19:00, 7 pm today | It means in 19 hours' time | It means 9 pm today | It means 9 am tomorrow
test: What would happen if a train timetable used a 12-hour clock without am or pm? | People could turn up 12 hours early or late | All trains would run at night | Trains would have to run twice as fast | Nothing at all would change
test: Leo watches a war film: "We move out at fourteen hundred." What time is that? | 2 pm | 4 pm | 14 minutes past 1 | 1400 minutes from now
exam: It is given that a train departs at 15:30. Ascertain the equivalent time upon a twelve-hour clock. | 3:30 pm | 5:30 pm | 3:30 am | 1:30 pm
exam: Identify the time at which midnight is expressed upon the twenty-four-hour clock. | 00:00 | 12:00 | 01:00 | 23:00
exam: Whereas an employment shift commences at 22:00 and terminates at 06:00, determine its aggregate duration. | Eight hours | Sixteen hours | Six hours | Four hours
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the abbreviation "pm". | It denotes a time after midday | It denotes a time before midday | It denotes midnight exclusively | It denotes the twenty-four-hour clock
